<title>GERMANY: Sick pay talks resume in Germany's Lower Saxony.</title>
<headline>Sick pay talks resume in Germany's Lower Saxony.</headline>
<dateline>HANOVER, Germany 1996-12-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>The German metal and engineering trade union IG Metall and employers surprisingly resumed on Wednesday talks in the state of Lower Saxony aimed at reaching a deal on new controversial sick pay wage laws.</p>
<p>Both sides said they hoped a solution could be found to the issue at a regional level after national talks collapsed in October that sought agreement on proposed sick pay cuts.</p>
<p>Union and employer officials said the talks in Lower Saxony were resumed on short notice with hopes a deal could be found.</p>
<p>It was not immediately clear how long the talks were scheduled to last for the some 90,000 metal workers in the north German state, according to officials at the talks.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
</text>
